   120	## 2. Task-quality gate (plan 0.2)
   121	- 8 fixed clips, 8.000 s each (= one 352800-sample chunk), from the
   122	  MUSDB18-HQ **test** split via the public HF mirror
   123	  `roro128/musdb18-hq-flac` at revision `32fd4e850b45ea0b635be067b921661984b53029`
   124	  (lossless FLAC re-encode of `danjacobellis/musdb18HQ`; CC BY-NC-SA 4.0;
   125	  research use only; **audio is never committed** — `configs/sdr_clips.json`
   126	  records track, split, clip start sample, and the sha256 of every derived
   127	  wav; `scripts/prepare_sdr_clips.py` regenerates the pack deterministically:
   128	  the first 8 tracks of shards `test-00000/00001` in shard order that have a
   129	  usable segment; clip start = the largest multiple of 441 ≤ 30 % of the
   130	  track length, advanced in 1-second steps until the 8-s **vocals** segment
   131	  has RMS ≥ 0.01 — a silent-vocals reference makes SDR degenerate; the first
   132	  draft of this rule, without the non-silence scan, produced two silent
   133	  clips and was corrected before registration).
   134	- **Every compared operating point is gated, paired against A at that same
   135	  operating point.** Reconstruction policy: each 8.000-s clip is processed as
   136	  non-overlapping consecutive chunks of the operating point's size (1×352800,
   137	  4×88200, 16×22050, 80×4410) and the chunk outputs are concatenated (no
   138	  overlap, no crossfade). Short-chunk `SDR_ref` values are therefore NOT
   139	  quality claims about the model; the gate is the paired regression under the
   140	  identical policy.
   141	- Per clip and arm: separated vocals `est`; `SDR(ref, est) = 10·log10(‖ref‖²
   142	  / ‖ref − est‖²)` (plain, no BSS-eval projections — never presented as
   143	  MUSDB BSS-eval SDR) and `SI-SDR(ref, est)` (both signals zero-mean; `s =
   144	  ⟨est,ref⟩/‖ref‖²·ref`; `10·log10(‖s‖²/‖est−s‖²)`); stereo is flattened to
   145	  one vector; float64 accumulation; a perfect match (e.g. `SDR(A_out, A_out)`)
   146	  is reported as `inf` (the gate's non-finite rule is the SDR_ref-terms rule
   147	  stated below, which governs). Computed (a) vs the reference vocals stem and (b) vs the saved
   148	  eager-A output for the same clip at the same operating point
   149	  (`SDR(A_out, est)` — output fidelity).
   150	- **Gate (common to every arm compared on speed):** mean over the 8 clips of
   151	  `ΔSDR_ref = SDR_ref(arm) − SDR_ref(A) ≥ −0.1 dB`. The per-clip minimum,
   152	  SI-SDR and the vs-A numbers are reported alongside; the gate is the mean
   153	  and stays the mean. Tolerance tiers remain the numerical-equivalence check.
   154	- Membership: the eight tracks are distinct and are canonical MUSDB18 test-set
   155	  titles (the mirror's `test` split re-exposes the upstream 50-track test
   156	  set; names checked). The KJ vocals checkpoint's training data is not
   157	  established by its model card, so the gate claims **paired implementation
   158	  fidelity**, not held-out generalization.
   159	- Non-finite semantics: `10·log10(num/den)` with `num>0, den=0` is `inf`
   160	  (perfect match), `num=0` is `undefined` (zero reference / projection). The
   161	  gate uses the `SDR_ref` terms only: a paired row whose arm or A term is
   162	  non-finite makes the gate ungateable → FAIL. Output-fidelity terms vs A may
   163	  be `inf` (A vs itself by construction) and are kept and counted, never
   164	  averaged away.
   165	- The gate runs in restart `r1` of each arm at each operating point, AFTER
   166	  the timed region (the driver's lifecycle: settle → gate → validate → time →
   167	  SDR); A's per-operating-point outputs come from the session's reference
   168	  step, so no extra model builds are needed.
   169	
   170	## 3. Graph-deployment verification (plan 0.3)
   171	"Zero graph breaks" is not accepted as evidence of full capture. For C1, C3,
   172	H (Inductor cudagraph trees) and B, E, G (manual graphs), record per pass,
   173	from the node-level nsys export of session 2 (`cuda_api_trace` +
   174	`cuda_gpu_trace`, capture window = the 5a latency block):
   175	`cudaGraphLaunch` calls/pass, `cudaLaunchKernel`-family calls/pass outside
   176	graph replay (eager launches), memcpy/memset API calls/pass, distinct kernel
   177	names inside vs outside replay; plus, for the Inductor arms, the
   178	`torch._inductor` cudagraph counters (`cudagraph_skips` and reasons,
   179	`TORCH_LOGS=cudagraphs`) and the cudagraph-trees recorded-graph count.
   180	Launch counts include runtime AND driver entry points (`cudaLaunchKernel`,
   181	`cudaLaunchKernelExC`, `cuLaunchKernel`, `cuLaunchKernelEx`; `cudaGraphLaunch`
   182	/ `cuGraphLaunch`). This nsys version's GPU trace carries no graph-id column
   183	even under `--cuda-graph-trace=node`, so GPU events are attributed by
   184	CorrId correlation: a kernel whose CorrId belongs to a graph-launch API call
   185	is captured; to a kernel-launch call, eager; neither → UNRESOLVED (counted,
   186	never assumed captured). API calls are windowed by the CPU-side NVTX pass
   187	range, kernels by the GPU-projected range. Fourfold classification per pass: (i) captured
   188	model computation (kernels inside replay); (ii) uncaptured model computation;
   189	(iii) external input staging (cudagraph-trees copies of non-static inputs
   190	into the static pool — `_foreach_copy_`/copy kernels/`cudaMemcpyAsync`);
   191	(iv) other uncaptured copies/memsets. Also reported: recorded-graph count
   192	(Inductor counters), graphs visited per pass, replay launches per pass, and
   193	compile/recording counter stability across settle → validation → timing.
   194	An arm is **fully graphed** only with positive replay evidence (≥1 graph
   195	launch in EVERY captured pass) AND zero uncaptured model kernels AND zero
   196	unresolved kernels in every pass (medians hide a defective minority); the
   197	captured pass count must equal the requested 50. Multiple graphs per pass
   198	are legitimate; staging (`_foreach_copy_`/multi_tensor_apply, direct-copy
   199	kernels, memcpy) is timed overhead but not uncaptured model work; bare
   200	elementwise kernels are model work.
   201	Arms verified: C1, C3, H (Inductor) and B, E, G (manual), from their
   202	session-2 traces at both operating points. No expectation is registered.
   203	
   204	## 4. Boundary-cost microbenchmark (plan 0.4; design registered, owner reviews)
   205	`fullmodel/boundary_bench.py`: chains of N **dependent** kernels, N ∈ {10,
   206	100, 1000, 2000}: (trivial) one Triton program loads ONE int32 element, adds
   207	1, stores it back to the same buffer — kernel k+1 depends on kernel k, and
   208	the final counter must equal the total launch count (exact-integer
   209	dependency proof); (elem37) an in-place value-preserving fp16 elementwise op
   210	on a 37 MB tensor, N ∈ {10, 100}; (gemm) alternating dependent GEMMs
   211	660×384×1536 / 660×1536×384 fp16 into preallocated ping-pong outputs with
   212	1/√K-scaled weights (bounded values), N ∈ {10, 100}. Modes: stream launches;
   213	one CUDA graph of the chain (replayed); programmatic dependent launch for the
   214	trivial chain (Triton 3.4 `launch_pdl=True`; `gdc_wait()` before the
   215	dependent load, `gdc_launch_dependents()` after the store; the PTX is checked
   216	for `griddepcontrol`), on a stream and inside a graph; real-kernel PDL is
   217	UNSUPPORTED through cuBLAS/ATen launches and recorded as such. Note: the
   218	triton 3.4.0 wheel's `language/extra/cuda/gdc.py` carries the pre-3.4
   219	`_builder=` signature and fails to compile under the 3.4 code generator
   220	(`_semantic=`); the bench therefore defines the same two one-line PTX
   221	intrinsics with the 3.4 signature (identical asm strings) — recorded here
   222	so the deviation from the library call is explicit. The elem37 chain's
   223	working set (37 MB) fits the RTX 5090's 96 MiB L2, so that chain measures
   224	the L2-resident rate, which is reported as the plan-1.4 L2 bandwidth input. Timing:
   225	unprofiled device time via CUDA events around the chain, median of 20
   226	repeats with min/max → `T(N)` per (chain, mode), plus least-squares slope
   227	and intercept over N. **`T(N)/N` and the slope are effective per-node service
   228	times INCLUDING kernel execution — not boundary costs.** A separate
   229	node-level trace of the same script yields, per chain segment (NVTX), the
   230	median kernel duration and the median serial inter-kernel gap; for non-PDL
   231	modes the serial gap is the boundary estimate; for PDL modes kernels overlap
   232	and no subtraction is attempted (pure boundary `UNRESOLVED`); the traced
   233	run's perturbation vs the unprofiled `T(N)` is reported. Prior calibration
   234	points to compare against: Hazy (H100 ≈ 2.1 µs stream, ≈ 1.3 µs graph);
   235	NVIDIA's CUDA 12.6 figure ≈ 0.55 µs/node is empty-kernel graph device
   236	service time (RTX 3060), not CPU launch cost. **Until measured, no
   237	per-boundary number is used anywhere**, and the ledger's X10 must not count
   238	kernel execution as removable boundary overhead. Trace-derived gaps are
   239	accepted only for segments whose qualification case exists in the traced
   240	run's own JSON with a passed dependency proof and PTX verdict and whose
   241	NVTX segment holds exactly N × 20 kernels (a missing qualification file
   242	yields no calibration value at all); the perturbation runs (plan 1.6) are
   243	accepted only when all three runs are VALID, validation PASS, one block
   244	holding exactly 50 recorded passes, manifest-bound with the same recorded
   245	effective variant, and (compiled arms) affirmatively recording-stable —
   246	otherwise EVERY derived ratio and the caveat are cleared to UNRESOLVED. Any
   247	unavailable mode is recorded `UNTESTED(reason)`.
   248	
   249	## 5. Session structure, protocol, budget
   250	- **Protocol 5a**, reused from `arms/common.py::timed_run` (per-iteration
   251	  synchronize latency block of 50 passes inside the cudaProfilerApi range;
   252	  one free-running block of 50 passes outside it), **five repeats per
   253	  process**; per-process statistic = min-of-medians and median spread; per
   254	  (arm × chunk) headline = **median over 3 fresh-process restarts of the
   255	  per-process min-of-medians**, every per-iteration list preserved
   256	  (an explicit extension of `sm120` README deviation 5; spread = max − min of
   257	  the repeat medians; within-process and between-process variation are both
   258	  preserved). **Plan 1.2 matched comparisons** use `fullmodel/matched_run.py`:
   259	  two arms built, settled and validated in ONE fresh process, then their 5a
   260	  blocks ALTERNATE (a, b, a, b, …, five each) so every block pair is matched
   261	  in time and clocks; repeated in three fresh processes. Registered pairs at
   262	  352800: (C1, C3) the graph-break tax; (C1, C1D) the DC-filter repair
   263	  alone; (C3, H) the pad-and-batch delta; (C1, C1A) once, to record the
   264	  expected compile failure. The matrix restarts are additionally round-major
   265	  (r1 of every arm, then r2, then r3). Clocks logged in the background at
   266	  1 Hz with timestamp and GPU UUID (`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=timestamp,uuid,
   267	  clocks.sm,clocks.mem,power.draw,temperature.gpu,utilization.gpu -l 1`) for
   268	  the whole session; timed blocks carry wall timestamps.
   269	  **Matched-pair acceptance:** both builds OK, both graph gates PASS, no
   270	  capped settle, a JOINT settle of the actual alternation (counters stable
   271	  across two full alternation rounds), both validations PASS after the joint
   272	  settle, counters stable across every block, and — joined at collection —
   273	  SDR PASS for both arms' cells at that operating point; otherwise the pair
   274	  is ineligible with reasons.
   275	  **Compile/recording stability:** a compiled run is eligible only if, across
   276	  settle → validation → timing → SDR, there are no new graph breaks or
   277	  unimplemented ops, the unique-graph count is unchanged, and every Inductor
   278	  cudagraph counter (recordings, skips, non-static-input recordings) is
   279	  unchanged; all snapshots are preserved in the run JSON.
   280	  **Provenance gate:** every process verifies the msst clone (HEAD = pin,
   281	  clean), the checkpoint sha256, the registered config path and the
   282	  explicit `use_amp` before building; the session preflight writes ONE
   283	  immutable `session_manifest.json` (repo commit + clean tree, sha256 of
   284	  this registration, of every measuring file, the clip manifest, pins,
   285	  environment) that every run and every reference is bound to; a mismatch
   286	  refuses the run.
   287	  **Headline rule:** a cell publishes a headline only if restarts r1, r2 AND
   288	  r3 all exist and are eligible (EXACTLY the 5×50 protocol, validation PASS,
   289	  recording state stable, manifest-bound, not a planted/traced/attribution
   290	  run), share ONE session manifest and ONE effective variant (capture scope
   291	  / adapter / compile mode / stream count), and the cell's SDR qualification
   292	  — taken only from the r1 record, same manifest and variant — is PASS; a
   293	  failed required restart is never replaced by a later tag. A matched pair
   294	  publishes a pair headline only if m1, m2 AND m3 are pair-eligible (5×50,
   295	  three matched restarts) under one manifest with one variant pair and both
   296	  arms' cells hold that SDR qualification; the matched runner re-checks each
   297	  arm's recording state after the joint settle and after validation.
   298	  **Recording observer:** compiled runs snapshot the cudagraph-trees
   299	  manager identity, the identity set of its recorded graph nodes (roots and
   300	  descendants) and its function-id set together with the dynamo/inductor
   301	  counters at every phase boundary; stability means every one of these is
   302	  unchanged (sets compared, never counts); a missing observer leaves
   303	  stability UNRESOLVED and the run ineligible.
   304	  **Independent qualification:** every publisher (driver, matched runner,
   305	  collector, completeness audit) re-derives eligibility from the record
   306	  itself through one versioned validator (`fullmodel/qualify.py`); stored
   307	  eligibility flags are never trusted, and a record whose stored flag
   308	  contradicts the validator is ineligible. Each run persists the sha256 of
   309	  every reference artifact its lineage check saw; the three restarts of a
   310	  cell must have been validated against the same hashed reference set.
   311	  **Runtime pins and manifest scope:** every process checks torch ==
   312	  2.8.0+cu128 and triton == 3.4.0 before building; the manifest binds the
   313	  complete measuring set (fullmodel/**, arms/*, scripts/*, configs/*,
   314	  frontend.py, frontend_rms_fused.py, setup.sh, this registration, the
   315	  runtime identity) and is compared field by field on every load; every
   316	  reference artifact (validation outputs, eager-A clip outputs) carries its
   317	  own lineage sidecar (manifest id + sha256) checked before use.
   318	- **Unprofiled timing is the primary result.** Session-2 traces are for
   319	  mechanism; profiled and unprofiled numbers never share a ratio. Profiling
   320	  perturbation (plan 1.6): A, C3, G at 4410 and 352800, wall unprofiled vs
   321	  `--cuda-graph-trace=graph` vs `=node`; >5 % inflation → every mechanism
   322	  number from that trace carries the factor as a caveat.
   323	- **Session 1 (≤ 3 h, RunPod secure-cloud RTX 5090, pod name
   324	  `melband_profiling`, image `runpod/pytorch:1.1.0-rc.154-cu1281-torch280-ubuntu2204`
   325	  = the pinned torch 2.8.0+cu128 / triton 3.4.0 matrix):** in this order —
   326	  setup (idempotent `setup.sh` + clip pack + nsys check); eager-A references
   327	  at both operating points; the two planted-fault checks; C3 operator
   328	  qualification per frame count; the matched pairs (§5, plan 1.2); the arm
   329	  matrix at 352800 and 4410 (three round-major restarts, SDR gate in r1 at
   330	  both points); C2 last. Budget guards (registered): a hard session deadline
   331	  (3 h) with a 15-min extraction reserve — a cell that cannot start before
   332	  the reserve is `UNTESTED(deadline)` and the session is INCOMPLETE, never
   333	  salvaged by weakening validation or timing; per-process caps (25 min
   334	  compile arms, 15 min others); an **aggregate C2 allowance of 40 min**, and
   335	  a timed-out cold C2 attempt is not retried at that operating point (the
   336	  remaining restarts are `UNTESTED`; a timeout means unavailable
   337	  performance, not evidence that C2 is slow; a C2 cell whose three restarts
   338	  are terminal `UNTESTED` records citing the allowance / no-retry / timeout
   339	  rule is permitted terminal unavailability and does not make the session
   340	  INCOMPLETE — every other missing or failed registered item does). Every run's JSON carries
   341	  `headline_eligible` with machine-readable reasons; a headline per (arm ×
   342	  operating point) requires three complete eligible restarts with the same
   343	  effective variant (capture scope / adapter / compile mode), else the cell
   344	  is `INCOMPLETE`. At the end of each session `fullmodel/completeness.py`
   345	  audits the registered mandatory items BY CONTENT — session 1: the 21
   346	  matrix cells, the three matched-pair families (m1–m3), the once-only
   347	  (C1, C1A) attempt's terminal record, both planted-fault records (FAIL at
   348	  the planted magnitude), the C3 operator qualification (all four frame
   349	  counts PASS); session 2: the eight sweep cells, the 19 node-level traces
   350	  (traced run VALID, one block of 50 passes, manifest-bound, exports
   351	  complete), graph verification and timing views per traced arm, ideal
   352	  costs (C3 requires a unique variant and validated windows), the 18
   353	  perturbation runs and six resolved ratios, source-map coverage for C3,
   354	  the attention bench (eight cases, model-layout ratios established), the
   355	  boundary bench (terminal status per registered chain) and its qualified
   356	  trace analysis; both sessions: manifest, collection summary, reference
   357	  lineage (directory marker and every sidecar), clock log, and every
   358	  auxiliary terminal record OK — and writes the mechanical session verdict
   359	  (COMPLETE / INCOMPLETE with per-item reasons) to `session_status.json`.
   360	  Collection and the audit are themselves supervised; if either fails the
   361	  session record is an explicit INCOMPLETE. Every on-pod reduction (nsys
   362	  exports, graph verification, timing views, ideal costs, perturbation) is
   363	  bounded by the session deadline, invalidates its stale outputs before the
   364	  attempt, and leaves a terminal `aux_<name>.json` (OK / FAILED /
   365	  UNTESTED); supervisor messages go to stderr. Pod setup (image boot,
   366	  `setup.sh`) precedes the session clock. Evidence extraction (validated
   367	  archive, receipt bound to the pod id) before terminate.
   368	- **Session 2 (≤ 3 h):** eager-A references + SDR at the sweep points; chunk
   369	  sweep (A, C3, G, H × {22050, 88200}, 3 restarts, SDR gate in r1);
   370	  node-level traces (ONE captured 50-pass block per traced run, outer NVTX
   371	  `pass_<i>` ranges, captured pass count checked against the requested 50
   372	  before attribution; generated-code dump from the SAME process for compiled
   373	  arms) of A, A-NVTX, C3, G, H **and C1, B, E, F** (graph verification §3;
   374	  timing views, plan 1.7) at both operating points, plus **C2 at 4410 only**
   375	  (the §6 comparator point; a C2 trace at 352800 is not budgeted);
   376	  profiling-perturbation runs (A, C3, G: one matching block each,
   377	  unprofiled vs `--cuda-graph-trace=graph` vs `=node`, reduced to ratios
   378	  with the >5 % caveat flag by `fullmodel/perturbation.py`);
   379	  attention-backend microbench (plan 1.5: time-axis batch 60 × seq T and
   380	  freq-axis batch T × seq 60, heads 8, dim_head 64, fp16, default scale,
   381	  dropout 0; TWO layout families — the model's actual SDPA input layouts
   382	  read from the C3 generated code (q/k as (b,n,h,d)-contiguous rotary
   383	  outputs viewed as (b,h,n,d); v a strided view into the fused qkv output)
   384	  and, as a separate experiment, contiguous (b,h,n,d); flash / efficient /
   385	  cuDNN / math forced one at a time, fp32 MATH-forced reference, CUDA-graph-
   386	  replayed device timing, unsupported cases recorded; the model's deployed
   387	  backend established PER operating point AND axis from the C3 node traces
   388	  — the 12 attention executions per pass in (time, freq) × 6 order, Triton
   389	  preprocessing kernels excluded — and a case whose deployed backend is not
   390	  established from a trace has NO ratio and is UNRESOLVED, never assumed);
   391	  graph verification requires an affirmative node-level export record and
   392	  the registered pass count (else its verdict is UNRESOLVED); timing views
   393	  count kernels only (memcpy/memset rows are reported separately as memops);
   394	  boundary microbench (§4) plus its node-level trace and traced-vs-unprofiled
   395	  perturbation; per-kernel ideal costs (plan 1.4) computed only from the
   396	  compiled variant(s) whose ORDERED per-pass Triton sequence is reproduced
   397	  by a UNIQUE decomposition into compiled-graph call sequences (graphs may
   398	  repeat; graphs are interchangeable only when their complete pricing
   399	  semantics agree — kernel body hashes, access sites, iteration literals,
   400	  argument shapes/strides/types and every extern/fallback call in order; graphs without Triton kernels are invisible to the
   401	  decomposition and are stated as such; SDPA call count reconciled exactly,
   402	  GEMM events ≥ extern calls, captured pass count = 50; zero or several
   403	  decompositions → UNRESOLVED, expected for graph-broken arms such as C1/C2
   404	  whose per-graph kernel numbering makes names non-unique). Inductor GEMM
   405	  template kernels (`triton_tem_*`, max-autotune) are counted in the GEMM
   406	  class and are not priced by the byte model. Registered deviations from a literal
   407	  per-kernel FLOPs/bytes table: cuBLAS kernel names carry no shapes, so GEMM
   408	  ideals are computed per extern call site from the wrapper and compared at
   409	  class level; SDPA ideals are class-level (12 calls/pass at the model
   410	  shapes); Inductor fused pointwise/reduction kernels are priced by bytes
   411	  only (bandwidth bound, zero FLOPs) with per-call L2 residency tests. Byte
   412	  model for a fused kernel call: per pointer argument, element size from the
   413	  kernel's own ABI signature (`triton_meta`), traffic = min(distinct
   414	  elements of the argument's strided footprint, load/store sites × the
   415	  call's iteration space) × element size, reads and writes charged
   416	  separately, where the iteration space is the product of the call's
   417	  positional integer literals bound one-to-one to the kernel's numeric
   418	  parameters (any other call form is UNRESOLVED); the L2 residency test
   419	  uses the union of the distinct base allocations the call touches, each at
   420	  its full allocated size (an over-estimate: a call passes the test only
   421	  when its whole working set provably fits); every measured value comes
   422	  from the validated per-pass windows (the aggregate kernel summary is only
   423	  reconciled against them), and an arm whose windows are not the registered
   424	  50 passes publishes no rows; graph-input dtypes from the wrapper's benchmark section;
   425	  fallback-op outputs from their `assert_size_stride`. This over-counts
   426	  slice reads, so a row whose HBM-model ideal exceeds its measured time is
   427	  flagged `bound_not_tight` and its recoverable share under the HBM model is
   428	  0 (never negative, never inflated); eager ATen fallbacks, cuFFT and copy
   429	  kernels without wrapper shapes stay UNRESOLVED. A kernel → fused-op source
   430	  map with its coverage share accompanies the table; exports; evidence
   431	  extraction. Session 2 runs under the
   432	  same deadline/reserve/terminal-record rules as session 1 (qualification
   433	  and microbenchmarks included).
   434	  **Plan 1.7 dependency model (registered):** the exports carry no event or
   435	  graph edges, so the critical-path view is the OBSERVED-ORDER longest chain
   436	  of pairwise non-overlapping kernels (edge a→b iff a ended before b
   437	  started); it is an upper bound on the true dependency path, satisfies
   438	  `critical_path ≤ union ≤ span` by construction (asserted), and is labelled
   439	  as such; the union and the summed views are exact.
   440	- Engineering shakedown of the harness on ml-beast GPU 4 is permitted before
   441	  session 1 (the `sm120` smoke rule): its outputs are never session evidence
   442	  and are never cited.
   443	- Budget guard: every process runs under `timeout`; a timed-out or crashed
   444	  run is INVALID/UNTESTED in its JSON and the session continues (loud in the
   445	  console log, never silent).
